.TH SCENE 2
.SH NAME
scene \- 2-D scene model, camera and renderer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "scene.m";
scene := load Scene Scene->PATH;
Model, Cam, Trails, Obj: import scene;

Model.new:     fn(): ref Model;
Model.read:    fn(dir: string): ref Model;
m.resolve:     fn();
m.apply:       fn(rec: string): string;
m.set:         fn(kind: int, id: string, attrs: list of (string, string));
m.del:         fn(kind: int, id: string): int;
m.find:        fn(kind: int, id: string): ref Obj;
m.objs:        fn(kind: int): array of ref Obj;
m.dump:        fn(): string;
m.extent:      fn(): (int, real, real, real, real);

Cam.new:       fn(r: Draw->Rect): ref Cam;
c.fwd:         fn(m: ref Model, a, b: real): Draw->Point;
c.inv:         fn(m: ref Model, p: Draw->Point): (real, real);
c.pan, c.zoomby, c.zoomat, c.fit, c.upp;
c.text:        fn(m: ref Model): string;
c.parse:       fn(s: string): int;

Trails.new:    fn(): ref Trails;
t.note:        fn(m: ref Model);

init:    fn(d: ref Draw->Display, f: ref Draw->Font);
render:  fn(dst: ref Draw->Image, m: ref Model, c: ref Cam,
             tr: ref Trails, flags: int, hud: string);
hit:     fn(m: ref Model, c: ref Cam, p: Draw->Point, radius: int): string;
signature:    fn(dir: string): string;
subsignature: fn(m: ref Model): string;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Scene
holds the semantics of the scene contract in
.BR docs/scene-design.md ;
.IR scenefs (4),
.IR scene (1)
are thin clients of it.
.PP
A
.B Model
is a set of objects of three kinds
.RB ( Scene->ENT ,
.BR FEAT ,
.BR LAYER ),
each the attribute list its producer wrote, kept in order, plus fields
parsed from it for drawing; its
.B meta
names the frame
.RB ( GEO :
positions are latitude, longitude;
.BR XY :
x, y with y up).
.B Read
loads a scene directory, and the scenes its scene layers
.RB ( kind=scene ,
.BR dir= )
name, resolved in the caller's namespace;
.B resolve
rereads just those;
.B apply
applies one record
.RB ( "time" ,
.BR ent ,
.BR feat ,
.BR layer ,
.BR meta ,
.BR del ,
.BR clear )
and returns an error string or nil;
.B dump
returns records that rebuild the model from empty.
.B Gen
changes on every change.
.PP
A
.B Cam
maps frame coordinates to pixels within its rectangle
.BR r :
.B zoom
is log2 of the scale (256\(mu2^zoom pixels per world in the geo frame,
2^zoom pixels per unit in xy).
.B Text
and
.B parse
convert to and from the
.B view
line of
.IR scenefs (4).
.PP
.B Trails
remembers entity positions each time
.B note
is called, for entities with a
.B trail=
length (or the model's default).
.PP
.B Render
draws the scene into
.B c.r
of
.IR dst :
layers or the default grid
.RB ( RGRID ),
features, trails, entities and labels, placed so they do not overlap,
then the HUD
.RB ( RHUD )
and zoom buttons
.RB ( RCHIPS ,
located by
.BR chiprects ).
Shapes are anti-aliased: they are paths, filled and stroked by
.B Image.fillpath
and
.B Image.strokepath
(see
.IR draw-image (2)).
.B Hit
returns the entity nearest
.IR p ,
or nil.
.PP
Layers draw in name order under the scene's own objects; a scene layer
is drawn with the same camera, blended at its
.BR opacity ,
and its labels are placed with everyone else's.
Objects with
.B hide=1
are not drawn.
.PP
.B Signature
hashes what the renderer would draw from a directory, scene layers
included, so a viewer can tell when to reread;
.B subsignature
covers only a model's scene layers.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/scene.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR scene (1),
.IR scenefs (4),
.IR draw-intro (2)
